// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`prompt-engineer handoff template snapshots > 'duo' 'planner' → 'prompt-engineer' renders the complete contract 1`] = `
"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`prompt-engineer\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="prompt-engineer"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`planner\` → \`prompt-engineer\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

The automatically injected \`<user-message>\` is the authoritative request. Pass the agent behavior the prompt must produce, stop producing, or hand off differently; the roles and teams affected, with the responsibility each role owns and the context each role receives through its handoff; observed agent output that motivates the change (quote only the lines that carry the signal and redact secrets as \`<REDACTED>\`); the prompt files or symbols already suspected; and constraints (prior decisions, the specialist decisions already accepted). Ask the prompt engineer for a prompt-change brief only: do not edit files, run builds, tests, or snapshot updates, or publish plans.

### When to use the prompt engineer

Use it when agent-facing prompt text (role identity, role guidance, team context, handoff templates, or routing and delegation prose) needs a design decision:
- a new role's prompt text;
- a rewording that changes what an agent does or hands off;
- an edit to prompt text that several roles or teams render.

Skip it for a mechanical edit of already-decided text: a typo, a renamed identifier, or a snapshot update for agreed text. When the change also alters a handoff contract, API, or schema, \`architect\` designs that first.

After designing, the prompt engineer must return the prompt-change brief to \`planner\` with the normal handoff command.

\`\`\`bash
chatroom handoff --chatroom-id="<chatroom-id>" --role="planner" --next-role="prompt-engineer" << 'CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END'
---MESSAGE---
[Your message here]
CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END
\`\`\`

### Grounding reference
Add this section to your message. Replace the placeholder with the id of the newest grounding message you published for this request, or write \`None.\`.

\`\`\`markdown
## Grounding
Grounding ID: <groundingId>, or \`None.\`
Read it: \`chatroom grounding read --chatroom-id="<chatroom-id>" --role="prompt-engineer" --grounding-id="<groundingId>"\`
It records factual observations from an earlier pass.
\`\`\`"
`;

exports[`prompt-engineer handoff template snapshots > 'duo' 'prompt-engineer' → 'planner' renders the complete contract 1`] = `
"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`planner\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="planner"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`prompt-engineer\` → \`planner\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

Instructions (not part of the message): Return one complete prompt-change brief for the authoritative request. The prompt engineer is advisory: do not edit files, run builds, tests, or snapshot updates, or publish plans. Decide the wording yourself; do not hand \`planner\` alternative drafts. Cite concrete paths and symbols. If nothing should change, or the requested change would make a prompt contradict itself or another rule, say so in \`## Summary\`, write exactly \`No change proposed.\` under \`## Proposed text\`, and explain why. Use the exact sections below, in this order.

## Summary
<state the behavior change in two to four sentences and whether text is proposed>

## Roles and responsibilities
<one line per role the change touches: the role, the responsibility it owns, and the outcome it hands to another role>

## Cross-role interactions
<the high-level sequence: which role produces work for which other role, in what order, and what each interaction must carry; write exactly \`None observed.\` when the change crosses no role boundary>

## Handoff context audit
<for each receiving role: the context it actually gets through the handoff, how it learns the decisions made before it, and where an assumption about missing context can start; write exactly \`None observed.\` when the change crosses no role boundary>

## Prompt surfaces affected
<one line per changed path: the path, the unit that owns it (for example a team folder or a shared module), and its kind (role identity, role guidance, team context, handoff template, routing or delegation prose)>

## Proposed text
<for each path: the current excerpt, then the exact proposed text, ready to paste; concise, each requirement stated as a positive, specific instruction, with a negative rule only for a strict must-avoid case>

## Structured handoff updates
<for each grounding document or handoff template that must change: its path, the section names to add, remove, or reword, and the content each receiving role needs; write exactly \`None required.\` when no information must survive a role change>

## Audit test
<the cross-role test to add or update: its path, the roles and handoff order it encodes, the context each role receives, and the false assumption it must fail on; write exactly \`None required.\` when the change crosses no role boundary>

## Cross-team effect
<each team and role whose rendered prompt changes, and why; an edit to one team's own prompt must change no other team; write exactly \`None observed.\` when only the named unit changes>

## Snapshots and guards affected
<each snapshot or test that changes or must stay unchanged, and the import or isolation rule the change must respect>

## Acceptance criteria
<testable statements, one per bullet, each naming the rendered prompt and the text it must or must not contain>

## Open questions
<only a product-scope question for \`product-manager\`, a contract or schema question for \`architect\`, or an item that meets the escalation criteria for \`user\`; write exactly \`None observed.\` if none remain>

## Work completed
<paths, decision records and documentation, and snapshots inspected, with what each showed>

Instructions after the body (not part of the message): run the normal handoff command to \`planner\` with the complete body above, then stop.

\`\`\`bash
chatroom handoff --chatroom-id="<chatroom-id>" --role="prompt-engineer" --next-role="planner" << 'CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END'
---MESSAGE---
[Your message here]
CHATROOM_HANDOFF_END
\`\`\`"
`;

